<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1906c470-2279-46f2-9eb3-7b53ea56814d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/af21db11-72c4-46bf-bef0-288336177ffa> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1b3b494d-9cb4-46d2-b595-58a0cde912f8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1b3b494d-9cb4-46d2-b595-58a0cde912f8> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Rule faithfulness is proved against slop's exact push model" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1b3b494d-9cb4-46d2-b595-58a0cde912f8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1b3b494d-9cb4-46d2-b595-58a0cde912f8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "SPEC §15 Q7 is settled: each loop-free completion rule states a `sound`/`complete` pair of @property contracts and `slop verify` proves them. This covers the seven loop-free rule functions: CR1, CR2, CR3, CR4 and CR5 on an arriving edge, and CR6 both ways, 15 properties in all. It needs slop 0.2.3 plus slop-lang/slop PRs #168 and #172, which the HOWL session wrote upstream. The four loop rules (CR4 and CR5 on an arriving subsumer, CR7 both ways) stay owed.\n\nWhy fix the verifier rather than work around it: on slop 0.2.3 the soundness direction came back unknown and completeness failed. The verifier translated a body as one expression and never modelled `list-push`. The user chose fixing the root cause upstream over witness-returning rules or leaving the contracts owed.\n\nThe contract form was forced by what the prover can express:\n- Premises use the body's own `node-eq`, `role-eq` and `set-has` calls, which the prover treats as opaque predicates. So the pair proves the body faithful to its own tests, and \"`node-eq` is equality\" stays `types.slop`'s business.\n- Completeness is an `exists` over field equalities, since a `record-new` in a contract is a fresh value.\n- CR2's second side states the body's own case split, `(node-eq b a1)` as an extra disjunct. Stated as one disjunction, CR2's completeness didn't decide even at 120 s.\n- `emit-edge` states what each half says as @property, not @post. A @post is also a runtime check under SLOP_DEBUG, and slop#171 miscompiles a multi-payload match there.\n\nEvidence:\n- Each proved property stops verifying under a mutation of its rule's body: the wrong address in CR1; CR2's second branch dropped; CR3's target swapped; CR6's edge reversed.\n- slop suite: 888 passed.\n- HOWL `make verify`: the same 37 functions verified, none failed or unknown.\n\nUpstream, Codex reviews of #168 and #172 found eleven false-verification paths, all fixed and pinned. Three were pre-existing bugs on slop main:\n- quoted enum arms;\n- Float payloads;\n- tail record fields under a shadowing `let`.\n\nFiled alongside: #167 (Option/Result Float payloads), #169 (callee posts asserted outside their branch), #171 (the transpiler's multi-payload match)." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/1b3b494d-9cb4-46d2-b595-58a0cde912f8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
