<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Profile selection is whole-ontology membership in one profile" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Auto-selection picks the cheapest IMPLEMENTED profile that contains EVERY construct in the ontology — never a union of profiles, and never a per-axiom mix. If no implemented profile contains the whole ontology the run is partial, and WHICH profile it runs under is fixed by rule rather than left open: choose the one leaving the fewest logical axioms omitted, breaking ties by the fixed profile order (el < horn-shiq < sroiq).\n\nWhy the rule is mandatory rather than an optimization: without it two conforming implementations classify different sub-theories of the same input and return different findings — both sound, neither reproducible. That violates determinism ACROSS IMPLEMENTATIONS rather than across runs, which is the harder failure to notice.\n\nWhy \"escalate when constructs span fragments\" is wrong: EL++ and Horn-SHIQ are INCOMPARABLE. An ontology with both a role chain (v0, not v1) and an inverse (v1, not v0) is inside neither, so \"escalate\" would have an implementation pick one rung and silently drop the other's constructs. Calling that escalation is wrong until a rung actually CONTAINS the mixed language — which is v2's job, and one more reason the ladder needs a top.\n\nChoosing a profile weaker than the ontology needs makes HOWL incomplete for it and, critically for validation, able to MISS AN INCOHERENCE. So an out-of-profile construct is never cosmetic: it records the axiom in coverage.omitted, which no command may report as coherent. It may still report incoherent from that same run, since a derived contradiction is definitive whatever the coverage.\n\nThe seam is built in v0 with only `profile-el` behind it — a dispatch point left in place is far cheaper than one retrofitted later. The rungs differ only in their calculus (rule set plus normal form and saturation-state variant) while sharing the front end, the fixpoint driver and the output stage, so the rule set is the swappable unit. Note also that `auto` is the DEFAULT and is NOT a Profile: ProfileSelection is a union of (auto) and (explicit Profile), because typing the config field as Profile while calling auto the default is something the type cannot express.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §5.1 (Profiles are selectable), §5.2, §6.5, §9 (flag surface)." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/3eacfa8e-800e-452f-9547-e4ee85c2f45d> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
