<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/50b82b38-4887-426f-97b5-7980d62742c8> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/50b82b38-4887-426f-97b5-7980d62742c8> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Canonical order is a length-safe memcmp, not strlib.compare" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/50b82b38-4887-426f-97b5-7980d62742c8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/50b82b38-4887-426f-97b5-7980d62742c8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "HOWL declares its own `memcmp` FFI and builds `string-cmp` on it (src/canon.slop), rather than importing `strlib.compare` — and the reason is a live correctness bug, not preference.\n\n`strlib.compare` returns -1/0/1 and looks like exactly the right import, but it is implemented as `strcmp` over `(. a data)` (strlib.slop:804), which assumes NUL termination. A SLOP `String` is `{len, data}`, and every IRI HOWL orders is a slice into a parsed Turtle buffer where the byte after an IRI is the rest of the document, not a NUL. `strcmp` therefore runs past the IRI and orders two entries by whatever happens to follow them in the file. Two documents expressing the same ontology, serialized differently, would sort differently — an accidental input-dependence, which is precisely the determinism violation the ordering exists to prevent.\n\nDemonstrated rather than argued: `test-string-cmp-is-length-safe` builds `apple` as a 5-byte slice sharing storage with `applesauce`. `strcmp` semantics report the two EQUAL (silently merging two distinct IRIs in a sorted report); `string-cmp` uses the length and returns -1. Mutation-testing confirms the test has teeth — removing the length tiebreak turns it and the sort-determinism test red.\n\nShape choices that travel with it:\n- MERGE SORT over an index permutation, not insertion sort. The toolchain has no sort at all (not lib/std, not slop-rdf, not GROWL) and no `list-set`, so every sort rebuilds; insertion sort would be O(n²) in time AND allocation, which is fine for an omission list and hopeless for GO's 73,634 subsumptions. Sorting indices under a caller-supplied `(Fn (Int Int) Int)` also works around SLOP's lack of generics: each element type costs a six-line gather, and later front-end stages can sort their own types without canon.slop knowing they exist.\n- The merge takes from the left on ties, so the sort is STABLE. Without that, two elements a comparator calls equal would have their relative order decided by the recursion split — a function of length, reintroducing the input-order dependence.\n- Every union comparator orders BY TAG FIRST. For `Node` this is load-bearing: OWL permits punning, so `class-node(:x)` and `individual-node(:x)` carry the same IRI, and an IRI-only comparator would call them equal and collapse the two populations the tags exist to separate.\n\nScheduling note: this was OWED through M1 (classify.slop carried the note) on the grounds that `classify` still refused so no consumer could observe the lists. M0 cannot defer it — canonicalization is §6.3 step 1, and fresh-node ids, the equivalence star \"around the least\", n-ary disjointness pairs, and the hashed report all inherit the order from it.\n\nEvidence: src/canon.slop (470 lines, 8 Z3 contracts verified); src/classify.slop collect-unsatisfiable/collect-taxonomy now sort on the way out; SPEC.md §6.3 step 1, §6.8." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
