<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5bde42ef-92a1-4f07-9071-da1b498203e8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/07e8c6db-5390-49d8-9f25-445b94ce79da> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5bde42ef-92a1-4f07-9071-da1b498203e8> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/436b177d-ee84-471d-980c-2ef95c93bf98> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "ABox encoding: class assertions to GCIs, role assertions to edges" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "Class and role assertions are encoded ASYMMETRICALLY, and the asymmetry is essential rather than incidental. `C(a)` becomes the pre-normalization GCI `individual(a) ⊑ C`. `r(a,b)` becomes an EDGE emitted into Δ₀ via `emit-edge(individual(a), r, individual(b))`, plus `derived-sub(A)` to `individual(b)` for every A in ran_T(r) — NOT the GCI `individual(a) ⊑ ∃r.individual(b)`.\n\nWhy role assertions cannot go through the existential: `individual(b)` is an ordinary concept name, not a singleton, so `individual(a) ⊑ ∃r.individual(b)` says only \"some member of individual(b)\", and two assertions naming the same b may be witnessed by DIFFERENT members. The identity of a repeated individual name — intrinsic to the ABox, not a matter of equality between distinct names — is lost. The counterexample: DisjointClasses(C,D), range(r)=C, range(s)=D, r(a,b), s(c,b) is inconsistent, yet under the existential encoding the r-successor and s-successor can be different members, no proxy becomes unsatisfiable, and HOWL reports COHERENT. An earlier draft justified the existential form by noting nothing in a nominal-free fragment can force two DISTINCT individuals to be equal — true and irrelevant, since the failure is one name occurring twice.\n\nTwo consequences of the edge form that are not optional. Edges enter through Δ₀, never by direct write to succs/preds: CR6 and CR7 fire on edge deltas, so a stored-only edge would leave role hierarchies and chains applying to derived edges but not asserted ones. And ranges must be seeded at installation, because the published range-elimination rewrite touches existential GCIs only — an installed edge is not a GCI, so nothing else would put C into S(individual(b)).\n\nBoth encodings treat each individual as a concept name in a SEPARATE TAGGED NAMESPACE. OWL permits class/individual punning, so without the tag a class `:x ⊑ ⊥` and an unrelated individual `:x` merge into one context and the encoding manufactures a FALSE inconsistency.\n\nWhy it is in v0 at all: it is the feature that unblocks the first consumer. Under the stub HOWL replaces, every instance-bearing delta is inconclusive, so the whole promotion path is restricted to annotation-only changes.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §5.2 (ABox encoding and its counterexample), §6.2 (Node tagging, edge-pair invariant), §10 test 13 (mandatory regression fixture), §13." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/5cecf538-1be2-4f57-a979-888812c3b7d4> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasLifecycleStatus> "accepted" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
