<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/9470d4c9-e254-4b73-ba9e-d9b30ba51d35> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85e6f9fb-40ea-497b-b689-3074f1a4ad52> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#weighs> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/Alternative/d3ea0cfc-9b85-43d8-bb2b-699c35a3465f> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#ArchitecturalDecision> <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "HOWL iteration is a deterministic global round, not a per-worker count" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/ArchitecturalDecision/85f90bba-3140-46ac-aa9f-47d76dbd2064> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "The saturation driver advances in semi-naive rounds with a barrier: every context processes the messages it held at the start of round n, and messages produced during round n are delivered for round n+1. Round count is therefore a function of the input alone, so max-iterations cuts at the same point on every host at any worker count.\n\nWhy: the consumer's reasoner port imposes a binding determinism contract — byte-identical canonicalized input plus the same budget must produce an identical canonical report, with no wall clock, randomness, or network inside the engine call. The report is content-hashed into a verdict identity, verdicts are immutable append-only records, and they are re-confirmed later. A report that varies run-over-run breaks verdict identity and every guarantee built on it.\n\nThe precise locus of risk (this is the non-obvious part): EL saturation is CONFLUENT, so a run that reaches the fixpoint derives the same conclusion set regardless of processing order — scheduling cannot change a completed answer. The entire exposure is in NON-COMPLETE outcomes. A resource-limit or cancelled run stops at whatever partial state the scheduler produced, and with ELK-style racing workers that state is a function of thread timing. Two identical runs at the same budget could report different unsatisfiable classes, or one could report complete and the other resource-limit — which under the run-status discipline is a different verdict, so it is a correctness bug rather than a performance wobble.\n\nRejected alternatives: (a) fold worker-count into the engine config hash — rejected because verdicts then stop being reproducible across deployments with different core counts, defeating the re-validation the fingerprint exists to enable; (b) a single-threaded deterministic mode for the port seam — rejected because it sacrifices the context-parallelism that is HOWL's whole performance story, kept in reserve if barrier cost proves prohibitive; (c) guarantee only that report fields are deterministic while raw saturation races — rejected as hard to establish and easy to violate silently.\n\nAccepted costs: barriers idle workers that finish a round early, so this is slower than pure ELK-style racing. That is the price of a decision-bearing cap. Cancellation is inherently timing-dependent and therefore returns no result payload at all. HOWL never consults a clock — wall-clock timeouts belong to the host, which pulls cancel-ptr, so a timeout can only ever be the host's inconclusive verdict and can never flip a pass to a fail.\n\nMust be settled before M0: retrofitting round semantics onto a message-passing driver is a rewrite, not a patch. Captured 2026-08-10." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
