<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasAuthor> "claude-code" <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
<https://moosedev.dev/kg/Constraint/65db656d-afe2-49bc-80aa-0d3677bca2e9> <https://trivyn.io/ontologies/software/architecture#hasDescription> "A run is COMPLETE exactly when `coverage.omitted` is empty AND `termination` is `fixpoint`. That conjunction — not a status tag — is what licenses a coherent verdict, a complete hierarchy, or a minimal repair.\n\nStatus is three INDEPENDENT axes, never one union:\n- `Coverage` — which axioms were processed. Empty `omitted` means full coverage; any Omission (out-of-profile, unresolved-import, missing-declaration) means the run saw less than the intended theory.\n- `Termination` — `fixpoint` or `resource-limit(n)`. Cancellation is NOT here: it yields `Fault::cancelled` with no Outcome, because a cancelled partial state is scheduler-dependent, cannot be made reproducible, and must never be serialized or hashed.\n- `Findings` — what was derived. Always present, always meaningful.\n\nThey are independent because a run can be both out-of-profile and resource-limited at once. The consumer's port models them as separate fields (`out_of_profile_axioms` alongside `fixpoint_reached`), and the mapping from a union could not be written without losing information — the consumer's data model was the check on ours.\n\nTHE VERDICT RULE, which is three-way and ASYMMETRIC:\n\n| Findings | Coverage + termination | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| incoherent | anything | INCOHERENT — definitive, even under partial coverage |\n| not incoherent | omitted non-empty, or termination ≠ fixpoint | INCONCLUSIVE |\n| not incoherent | omitted empty AND fixpoint | COHERENT |\n\nThe asymmetry between rows 1 and 2 is not a compromise; it falls out of MONOTONICITY. Every rule is faithful, so anything in findings is sound whatever the coverage; and restoring an omitted axiom can only ADD conclusions, never retract one, so a ⊥ derived from a subset of the ontology is a ⊥ in the whole ontology. A finding is definitive; the ABSENCE of a finding is what carries no information. An earlier draft sent every out-of-profile run to inconclusive, which discarded sound results.\n\nWhy the safety direction matters: HOWL's flagship service is `validate` as a CI gate. Several paths reach a non-fixpoint state, and a validator that silently returns \"coherent\" when it could not tell is worse than no validator, because CI trains users to trust it. Surfaces as exit code 2 (inconclusive), distinct from 0 (coherent) and 1 (incoherent); `--strict` controls only whether HOWL refuses to run versus returns a marked-partial result, and NO flag can promote an incomplete run to a verdict. The same monotonicity is why a partial run may never report a COMPLETE hierarchy or a MINIMAL repair — those are claims about absence, and a hitting set over an incomplete justification set is not minimal.\n\nThe port's fault precedence and the CLI's exit codes are both this table in their own dialects; neither may restate it differently.\n\nEvidence: SPEC.md §6.2 (Outcome, the verdict rule and its diagram), §6.7, §8.4 (fault precedence), §9 (exit codes), §10 item 4." <https://moosedev.dev/kg/project> .
